12. 10-Mark Exam Answer ⭐⭐⭐
Big Data and Database Evolution

Big Data refers to extremely large, complex, and rapidly generated datasets that cannot always be efficiently managed using traditional database systems alone. The growth of web applications, social media, IoT devices, sensors, mobile applications, and digital services has resulted in enormous amounts of data.

Database technology has evolved over time. Initially, data was stored using traditional file systems. Relational databases then introduced tables, relationships, SQL, transactions, and ACID properties. As data became larger and systems became geographically distributed, distributed databases and NoSQL databases became important. Big Data platforms such as Hadoop and Spark were developed to support distributed storage and processing of very large datasets. Cloud databases and modern data platforms further provide scalable and managed infrastructure.

Big Data is commonly described using the 5 Vs: Volume, Velocity, Variety, Veracity, and Value.

Distributed computing is an important part of Big Data. Large datasets can be divided across multiple machines, allowing them to be processed in parallel. This provides scalability, performance, and fault tolerance.

Evolution
File Systems
     ↓
Relational Databases
     ↓
Distributed Databases
     ↓
NoSQL Databases
     ↓
Big Data Platforms
     ↓
Cloud Databases
     ↓
Modern Data Platforms

Big Data technologies are useful for large-scale analytics, log processing, machine learning, IoT data, and other workloads involving very large datasets. However, traditional relational databases remain important for structured data and transactional applications.

13. MySQL Practical — LAST ⭐

