##### HDFS and Hive Overview
1. HDFS — Definition ⭐

HDFS (Hadoop Distributed File System) is a distributed file system used by Hadoop to store very large datasets across multiple machines.

HDFS divides large files into blocks and stores those blocks across multiple nodes with replication for fault tolerance.

Basic Architecture
              HDFS
                │
        ┌───────┴───────┐
        ▼               ▼
   NameNode        DataNodes
  (Metadata)      (Actual Data)
                      │
              ┌───────┼───────┐
              ▼       ▼       ▼
            Block1  Block2  Block3
Main Components
NameNode → Manages metadata, file names, directories, and block locations.
DataNode → Stores the actual data blocks.
Blocks → Large files are divided into blocks.
Replication → Copies of blocks are stored on multiple DataNodes for fault tolerance.
Advantages
Handles very large datasets.
Provides fault tolerance through replication.
Supports distributed storage.
Scales by adding more machines.
Suitable for Big Data workloads.
Disadvantages
Not ideal for very small files.
Requires multiple machines for distributed operation.
Mainly designed for high-throughput access rather than low-latency transactions.
2. Hive — Definition ⭐

Apache Hive is a data warehouse system built on Hadoop that allows users to query and analyze large datasets using HiveQL, a SQL-like language.

Hive makes Big Data analysis easier by providing a SQL-like interface over data stored in systems such as HDFS.

Basic Architecture
User
  │
  ▼
HiveQL Query
  │
  ▼
   Hive
  │
  ▼
HDFS / Hadoop Data
  │
  ▼
Query Result
Main Features
Uses HiveQL, which is similar to SQL.
Designed for large-scale data analysis.
Works with Hadoop storage.
Supports data warehousing and batch analytics.
Uses schema-on-read, meaning structure is applied when data is read.
Advantages
SQL-like language is easy to learn.
Suitable for large datasets.
Useful for data analysis and reporting.
Works with Hadoop ecosystem.
Supports schema-on-read.
Disadvantages
Not designed for real-time transactions.
Query execution can have higher latency.
Not suitable for OLTP applications.
3. HDFS vs Hive ⭐⭐⭐
HDFS	Hive
Distributed file system	Data warehouse/query system
Stores data	Queries and analyzes data
Uses DataNodes	Uses HiveQL
Manages distributed storage	Provides SQL-like data analysis
Part of Hadoop	Built for Hadoop ecosystem
4. Practical — Conceptual Only ⭐

These require a Hadoop/Hive environment, so they are not MySQL commands.

HDFS command
hdfs dfs -ls /

Lists files/directories in HDFS.

HiveQL example
SELECT department, COUNT(*)
FROM employees
GROUP BY department;

This is HiveQL, not MySQL SQL in this context.

